issue_context.body:
**Describe the bug**
In z_sched_abort of thread_abort.c
```
LOCKED(&sched_spinlock) {
			if (z_is_thread_prevented_from_running(thread)) {
				__ASSERT(!z_is_thread_queued(thread), "");
				thread->base.thread_state |= _THREAD_DEAD;
			} else if (z_is_thread_queued(thread)) {
				_priq_run_remove(&_kernel.ready_q.runq, thread);
				z_mark_thread_as_not_queued(thread);
				thread->base.thread_state |= _THREAD_DEAD;
			} else {
				k_busy_wait(100);
			}
		}
```
the sched_spinlock should be released before k_busy_wait(100),  if not released,  other cores cannot get the sched_spinlock during k_busy_wait(100).  This will worse the compete of locks, especially for cores >= 4

review_comments:
Style wise, I prefer to keep the lock and unlock pairs at the same level of indentation.  Can you change this to something like:

```c
    k_spin_unlock(&sched_spinlock, key);
    k_busy_wait(100);
    k_spin_lock(&sched_spinlock);
```

I did't find a way to keep the lock and unlock parirs at the same level.  In the provided codes, k_spin_lock cannot be after k_busy_wait  as while((thread->base.thread & _THREAD_DEAD)) may exit before call k_spin_unlock.   How about the following:

```
		LOCKED(&sched_spinlock) {
			if (z_is_thread_prevented_from_running(thread)) {
				__ASSERT(!z_is_thread_queued(thread), "");
				thread->base.thread_state |= _THREAD_DEAD;
			} else if (z_is_thread_queued(thread)) {
				_priq_run_remove(&_kernel.ready_q.runq, thread);
				z_mark_thread_as_not_queued(thread);
				thread->base.thread_state |= _THREAD_DEAD;
			}
		}
                k_busy_wait(100);
```
But the problem is that k_busy_wait(100) will be always executed
